WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.175
The compliance track is for the people
who read evidence rather than build it:

2
00:00:05.175 --> 00:00:09.900
I S S Os, compliance staff, control
assessors and authorizing officials.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.975
It teaches one skill
above the others, tracing.

4
00:00:12.975 --> 00:00:17.466
Given a control, name the mechanism
that closes it, the book that built it,

5
00:00:17.466 --> 00:00:22.262
the record it emits and where that record
sits in the authorization package.

6
00:00:22.262 --> 00:00:26.549
Given a record, work back to its
control and say whether it's current.

7
00:00:26.549 --> 00:00:29.481
The orientation module carries four ideas:

8
00:00:29.481 --> 00:00:33.878
the Risk Management Framework's
seven steps; who owes which proof,

9
00:00:33.878 --> 00:00:38.031
with only the authorizing
official accepting residual risk;

10
00:00:38.031 --> 00:00:41.369
Moderate and FedRAMP's Class
C as two facts, not one;

11
00:00:41.369 --> 00:00:46.825
and FedRAMP's forty-six key security
indicators binding providers, not agencies.

12
00:00:46.825 --> 00:00:52.050
Eight modules then follow the program's
evidence areas, from identity to training.

13
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.897
Each one teaches a discipline:
a policy screenshot can't show

14
00:00:55.897 --> 00:00:59.975
that a sign-in was phishing-resistant;
only the sign-in record can.

15
00:00:59.975 --> 00:01:04.030
The capstone is a continuous-monitoring
narrative for one system.
